      "answers": [
        "The reply includes an A address, so it must be authoritative.",
        "The NS reply has the aa flag and names ns1.novasolutions.com as an NS record.",
        "The recursive A reply includes the ra flag, so it must be authoritative.",
        "The resolver returns the same address twice, so it must own the DNS zone."
      ],
      "correctAnswer": 1,
      "persona": "Whitehat Hacker",
      "taskMessage": "<b>Brandon:</b> Compare the two DNS replies. The authoritative-answer flag tells us which response came from a server answering for the zone; a listed NS record by itself is not proof that the responding server was authoritative.",
      "taskInstructions": "In the command shell, run <b>dig novasolutions.com NS</b> and <b>dig novasolutions.com A</b>. Compare their header flags, question types, answer records, and server lines.",
      "taskQuestion": "Which observation indicates an authoritative answer?",
      "taskCompleteText": "<b>Brandon:</b> The NS reply carries <b>aa</b>, the authoritative-answer flag, and returns <b>ns1.novasolutions.com</b> as an NS record. The recursive A reply lacks <b>aa</b>. Neither result explains the traffic spikes."
    }
